[interviewer · INTRO] Your client is Brew & Bean, a specialty coffee chain with 200 locations across the US. Over the past two years, their net profit margin has declined from 24% to 6%, even though revenues have grown 15% over the same period. The CEO has hired us to identify the root cause of this profit decline and recommend how to reverse it. How would you approach this problem?

Take a minute if you need it, then tell me how you'd structure your approach.

[candidate silent 40s]

[candidate · 2:10] Let me lay out how I want to attack this. Rather than a revenue-and-cost tree, I want to build up from the single customer transaction, because that's the unit a coffee chain actually operates on, and then multiply out to the chain.

Total profit = (price per cup − cost per cup) × cups per store per year × number of stores.

So four buckets, and they're mutually exclusive:

One, price per cup — menu pricing, discounting and loyalty redemptions, mix across drinks and food.

Two, cost per cup — this splits cleanly into costs that scale with the cup, so coffee, milk, cups and lids, the labor minutes to make it; and costs that don't scale with the cup but get allocated across cups — rent, utilities, corporate overhead. That second piece matters because it's where a per-cup number can blow up if volume per store falls.

Three, cups per store — traffic and attach rate.

Four, number of stores — new openings carry drag before they mature.

Revenue is up 15% and margin collapsed, so I'd start on cost per cup.
