In absolute terms, indexing revenue at 100 then 115: COGS went from 42 to 58% × 115 ≈ 66.7, so up about 59% while revenue grew 15%. That's a real cost increase, not an allocation artifact — and notably it's steady year over year, not a one-time shock.

In my structure that sits squarely in cost per cup, specifically the inputs that scale with the cup: coffee beans, milk, cups and lids. Two drivers: input prices went up, or we're using more input per cup — bigger sizes, more milk-heavy drinks, waste, spoilage.

