[interviewer · EXHIBIT · 5:27] Coffee beans were 25% of COGS two years ago. Other input costs — dairy, packaging, and food — are up 37.5% over the past two years. Menu prices have not changed in two years. What do you make of those together, and what would you want to know next?

[candidate · 6:57] Let me do it per cup, since prices are flat — revenue up 15% with flat price means cups sold are up 15%.

Index cups at 100 going to 115. COGS per cup two years ago = 42/100 = 0.42. Today COGS = 58% × 115 = 66.7, over 115 cups = 0.58. So cost per cup is up about 38%.

Split it. Beans were 25% of 0.42 = 0.105 per cup; everything else 0.315. If that "everything else" is up 37.5%, it's now 0.315 × 1.375 = 0.433. Subtract from 0.58 and beans are now about 0.147 — roughly 40% up.

So this isn't one rogue input. It's broad input inflation of 38–40% across the board, and we have absorbed every cent of it because menu prices haven't moved in two years. Price per cup is frozen while cost per cup inflates — that's the root cause.

Next I'd want competitor pricing and any read on our price elasticity.
