[interviewer · BRAINSTORM · 8:37] Raw coffee bean costs are up 40% over the past two years.

[candidate · 10:07] That matches what I backed into exactly — I derived beans at roughly 40% from the residual, and you're telling me 40%. So the arithmetic holds and the picture is complete.

It also tells me something useful about causation: beans up 40%, everything else up 37.5%. Those are almost the same number. This isn't a Brew & Bean sourcing problem or a bad supplier contract — if it were, one line would spike and the others wouldn't. Broad, even inflation across beans, dairy, packaging and food is a market-wide input shock, which means our competitors are almost certainly facing the same cost curve.

That strengthens the pricing case considerably. If the whole category is absorbing 38–40% input inflation and we've held price for two years, we are very likely priced below where the market has already moved. The risk of raising price is lower than it would be if we were the only one doing it.

So: the problem is a price-per-cup problem, not a cost-per-cup problem we can fix by cutting.
