[interviewer · BRAINSTORM · 10:11] Okay. Beyond a price increase, what else could Brew & Bean do to reverse the margin decline? Give me the full set of ideas.

[candidate · 11:41] Let me run it through my four buckets so it's complete.

Price per cup — beyond a headline increase: raise price on the most inelastic items, espresso drinks rather than drip; shrink discounting and loyalty giveaways, which is a price increase customers feel less; mix-shift by pushing higher-margin items — food attach, larger sizes.

Cost per cup, the scaling part — hedge or lock forward contracts on beans and dairy so we stop taking spot prices; renegotiate or consolidate suppliers for volume terms; reformulate portions and sizes modestly; attack waste and spoilage; cheaper packaging, since lids and cups carry no brand value in a specialty position; substitute within the bean blend where the palate can't detect it.

Cost per cup, the non-scaling part — overhead crept 12 to 14, so there's a couple of points there, and renegotiate rent at renewal.

Cups per store — more volume spreads the fixed 14 points of overhead over more cups. Dayparts, afternoon traffic, catering.

Number of stores — if any locations are below contribution breakeven, close or don't renew them.

Pricing is the only lever sized to an 18-point gap.
