[interviewer · RECOMMENDATION · 11:45] You said pricing is the only lever sized to the gap. Walk me through that, including how a price increase would actually flow through to margin.

[candidate · 13:15] Here's why. Take the current state indexed: revenue 115, COGS 66.7, labor 25.3, overhead 16.1, profit 6.9.

A price increase is nearly pure flow-through. If volume holds, every extra dollar of price adds no COGS, no labor, no overhead — it drops straight to the bottom line. So a 10% price rise takes revenue to 126.5, costs stay 108.1, profit goes 6.9 to 18.4. Margin 14.5%. One price move roughly triples profit.

To fully restore 24%, costs of 108.1 have to equal 76% of revenue, so revenue needs to be 142.2 — about a 23.5% increase. That's steep, so I'd stage it.

Now compare the cost levers. Labor and overhead together are 36% of revenue; cutting 10% off both recovers 3.6 points and damages service. Supplier renegotiation on a market-wide shock might claw back a few percent of COGS — call it 2 points. Neither is close to 18.

And note the asymmetry: a 10% price rise needs volume to fall less than about 9% to be worth it. That's the number I'd test.
