[interviewer · RECOMMENDATION · 14:55] Walk me through the labor inference. Labor stayed at the same share of revenue while revenue grew, and you read that as labor being fixed. Does that reading hold up?

[candidate silent 7s]

[candidate · 16:32] No, it doesn't hold up. If labor were fixed, revenue growing 15% should have pushed labor's share down from 22 to about 19. Flat share means labor grew in lockstep with revenue, which is the signature of a variable cost, not a fixed one. I had it backwards — overhead is the one that behaved oddly, climbing 12 to 14, which is the opposite of fixed-cost leverage and worth a separate question.

Recalculating with labor variable. Contribution today = 115 − 66.7 − 25.3 = 23. Fixed overhead 16.1. With a 10% price rise, revenue 126.5v, variable costs 92v, contribution 34.5v. Set equal to 23: v = 0.667.

So breakeven volume loss is about 33%. That's an even wider cushion, because variable costs shrink with the lost volume.

The caveat is that labor is probably semi-variable — scheduled in shifts, not per cup. So true breakeven sits between 19% and 33%. Either way, comfortable.
