[interviewer · RECOMMENDATION · 16:37] Understood. Now give me your recommendation to the CEO: what should Brew & Bean do, and how would you sequence it?

[candidate · 18:07] Recommendation: raise menu prices. That's the answer — the margin decline is a pricing problem, not a cost problem, and pricing is the only lever sized to the gap.

The logic: COGS went 42 to 58% of revenue over two years, driven by 38–40% input inflation across beans, dairy, packaging and food, while menu prices have not moved once. Labor held at 22% and overhead at 12–14%, so operations aren't broken. We simply absorbed a market-wide cost shock instead of passing it through.

Sequencing. Immediately, a 10% increase weighted toward espresso drinks and food, which triples profit to roughly 14.5% margin if volume holds — and breakeven volume loss is somewhere between 19% and 33%, so there's real cushion. In parallel, cut discounting and loyalty giveaways. Over the next two quarters, test a second increase toward the ~23% needed to restore 24%, and lock forward contracts on beans and dairy to stop the bleeding repeating.

Risks: no competitor pricing and no elasticity read. So I'd pilot in 20 stores, measure transaction counts weekly, and roll out from there. If the category has already repriced, we're late, not early.
